9. Practical shared data and protected code
struct run {
  struct run *next;
};
struct {
  struct spinlock lock;
  struct run *freelist;
} kmem;
Figure 2. Shared allocator representation from the examined kalloc.c.
kmem.freelist points to the first available physical page. For each free page, next identifies the
next free page. The invariant requires a coherent chain of available pages: allocation removes
one page, and freeing adds one valid page. The normal list updates are protected by the same
lock in both functions.
/* kalloc(): protected pop */
acquire(&kmem.lock);
r = kmem.freelist;
if (r)
  kmem.freelist = r->next;
release(&kmem.lock);
/* kfree(): protected push */
acquire(&kmem.lock);
r->next = kmem.freelist;
kmem.freelist = r;
release(&kmem.lock);
Figure 3. Exact critical-section excerpts; surrounding validation and fills are omitted.
kfree() checks alignment and address range and fills the page before linking it. kalloc() fills the
removed page after releasing the list lock. These details explain why the protected section is
narrow. Locking does not make an invalid free correct; callers must still obey page ownership
and lifetime rules.

