10. Practical C workload and checking logic
The parent creates a pipe and forks four children. Each child waits for one byte from the pipe.
After creation, the parent writes four tokens so workers can overlap. This start gate encourages
concurrent requests; it does not guarantee simultaneous execution of any specific kernel
instruction.
for (int round = 0; round < ROUNDS; round++) {
  char *allocation = sbrk(bytes);
  /* Check allocation failure. */
  volatile uchar *memory = (volatile uchar *)allocation;
  for (int offset = 0; offset < bytes; offset++)
    memory[offset] = tag;
  /* Brief user-space delay. */
  for (int offset = 0; offset < bytes; offset++)
    if (memory[offset] != tag)
      /* Report corruption and fail this child. */;
  /* Shrink by bytes; check failure. */
}
Figure 4. Explanatory workload excerpt; Appendix A contains the complete executable file.
Each child allocates eight pages, writes its own tag value from 1 to 4 into every byte, waits
briefly in user space, verifies every byte, and releases the allocation. A changed byte generates a
CORRUPTION message containing the round, offset, expected value, and actual value. The
delay is not a kernel race-window modification.
The parent waits for child exit statuses. A successful result requires all four children to complete
and no setup failure. Allocation, shrink, pipe, fork, and wait errors are handled. volatile forces
the verification to reread memory in this program; it does not synchronize the allocator or repair
its race.
A private user buffer is the observation point, while the shared kernel allocator is the modified
component. Equal virtual addresses across processes are expected and do not prove that the
same physical page was allocated twice.

