14. Deliberate lock removal
The unlocked variant comments out exactly four allocator calls: acquire and release in kfree(),
and acquire and release in kalloc(). The free-list update statements and the guest workload
remain unchanged. The saved B-lock-removal.diff records the intended intervention.
kfree():
- acquire(&kmem.lock);
+ // acquire(&kmem.lock);
  r->next = kmem.freelist;
  kmem.freelist = r;
- release(&kmem.lock);
+ // release(&kmem.lock);
kalloc():
- acquire(&kmem.lock);
+ // acquire(&kmem.lock);
  r = kmem.freelist;
  if (r)
    kmem.freelist = r->next;
- release(&kmem.lock);
+ // release(&kmem.lock);
Figure 7. Focused excerpt of the saved allocator-lock removal diff.
After rebuilding, the runner performs B01-B10 with the same four-CPU stress program. The
variant intentionally violates the normal locking discipline. This is a controlled teaching
experiment rather than a proposed production optimization.
acquire() and release() also manage nested local interrupt state. Their removal changes that
behavior wherever an outer lock is not already active. Consequently the comparison concerns
the complete existing allocator locking discipline; it does not independently measure mutual
exclusion, ordering, and interrupt handling as separate interventions.
Keep the removal diff and original allocator copy with the results so the experiment is auditable.
Restoration follows this phase; the final project must retain its original protection.

